{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>I will not again curse the ground any more for the sake of man.</b> And so it was said to man (Genesis 3:17), אֲרוּרָה הָאֲדָמָה בַּעֲבוּרֶךָ ('cursed be the ground for your sake'). From here there is proof that the ground was a cause of man's sin, for it placed within him a coarse and thick matter that inclines more toward materiality, as it is written (Leviticus 18:25), ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ״ ('and the land became defiled, and I visited its iniquity upon it'). And this was said specifically concerning the seven nations that dwelt in the Holy Land aforetime, because even though the Holy One, blessed be He, gathered its dust from all four winds of the world, so that all lands have a share in man's sin, for all of them placed within him thick and coarse matter — nevertheless, all the other lands received their punishment and were purified by the waters of the Flood: the upper three handbreadths of the plowshare were ruined, and thereby the matter of the earth was softened and its hardness removed. And from then on the matter of the earth is not as evil as at first, whether from the side of the punishment, for it was purified by the waters of the Flood in the manner of every immersion, which is purification and removal of impurity and defilement, or from the side of nature, for the entire earth was softened in the waters of the Flood, and from then on the matter of the earth is not so hard as it was aforetime. But concerning the land of Israel it is said in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 22:24), ״אֶרֶץ לֹא מְטֹהָרָה הִיא וְלֹא גֻשְׁמָהּ בְּיוֹם זָעַם״ ('a land that is not cleansed, nor rained upon in the day of indignation'). Since it was not purified by the waters of the Flood, its defilement is still within it, and when the inhabitants of that land sin, one may attribute the sin to the land. Therefore it was said specifically concerning the lands of the seven nations, ״וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ וָאֶפְקֹד עֲוֹנָהּ עָלֶיהָ״ etc., for through this is resolved that which one may ask: behold, Scripture says (Leviticus 18:3), ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ etc. ״וּכְמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן״ etc. — this was compared to that, and if so, why would the land of Egypt not vomit out its inhabitants as the land of Canaan vomited out the nation? Rather, because the land of Egypt was rained upon in the day of indignation and one can no longer attribute their transgression to the land — if so, why should it vomit them out? For it is sufficient if the sinning nation receives its punishment upon its own land. But the land of Israel was not rained upon; one may still attribute it to the land, therefore it says (Deuteronomy 29:21), ״וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ״ etc., as will be explained in its place with the help of HaShem. Therefore, after this Flood, it says: ״לֹא אֹסִף עוֹד לְקַלֵּל אֶת הָאֲדָמָה בַּעֲבוּר הָאָדָם״ ('I will not again curse the ground any more for the sake of man'), for from here onward one may not hang man's transgression on the earth, but only on his evil inclination, which draws its strength from the serpent and from Samael its rider. This is why He gave the reason for the matter: ״כִּי יֵצֶר לֵב הָאָדָם רַע מִנְּעֻרָיו״ ('for the inclination of man's heart is evil from his youth'), that the principal cause of his sin shall depend on his inclination and not on the matter of the earth." "And the proof for this is that in the generation of the Flood it is written ״וְכָל יֵצֶר מַחְשְׁבֹת לִבּוֹ רַק רַע כָּל הַיּוֹם״ (Genesis 6:5), meaning that it went on diminishing from day to day. And this sin surely depends on the matter of the earth, not on the side of man's intellectual power, for ״בִּישִׁישִׁים חָכְמָה וְאֹרֶךְ יָמִים תְּבוּנָה״ (Job 12:12), and how could man not be reconciled from his wickedness upon arriving in years? Now, if the matter alone were its cause, this would be fitting, for the matter, by virtue of its bestial nature, even when it grows old does not depart from its folly. But after the Flood, when the matter of the earth was purified, from then on one may not attribute the sin except to the evil inclination, which overpowers more in the days of his youth; but when he grows old, the eye of his intellect opens and he does not return to folly. Therefore it is not written ״כָּל הַיּוֹם״ ('all the day'), but only ״מִנְּעוּרָיו״ ('from his youth'). Now, when he comes to thirteen years and his companion also comes and examines him — that is the good inclination — and this is the cause of their continued existence."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:54.196397452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}